"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 12 and positive surgical margins. The pathological findings show an ISUP grade group 2 (Gleason 3+4) with pT2 disease, but the presence of positive margins suggests local residual disease risk. A countervailing finding is the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymphovascular invasion, along with no documented lymph node metastasis. Furthermore, the biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows a grade downgrade from Gleason 3+3 (biopsy) to Gleason 3+4 (pathology), which may temper concern regarding aggressive local disease progression.\n\nInformation that would significantly revise this prognosis includes postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory; a rising or persistently elevated PSA warrants closer investigation. The current nodal status is documented as negative, but the lack of further follow-up data limits prognostic certainty. The fixed estimated horizon is 56.82 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.45–68.18 months; its coverage has not been validated."
